WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.589
Ten in the morning, halfway through a tray of rye, the bakery's oven breaks down.

2
00:00:07.589 --> 00:00:09.642
The packer has boxed a few loaves.

3
00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:14.048
Then nothing more comes down the belt, only a note: the oven has stopped.

4
00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:16.192
Let's shrink that.

5
00:00:16.192 --> 00:00:24.029
The program shares 8 loaves among some boxes, and the box counts come down the belt: 1, 2, 0 and 4.

6
00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:25.454
Eight over 1 is 8.

7
00:00:25.454 --> 00:00:26.879
Eight over 2 is 4.

8
00:00:26.879 --> 00:00:28.859
Then eight over 0 throws.

9
00:00:28.967 --> 00:00:30.531
What happens to the 4?

10
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:33.685
Nothing.

11
00:00:33.685 --> 00:00:36.827
The demo printed 8, then 4, then the error.

12
00:00:36.827 --> 00:00:38.507
The 4 is never divided.

13
00:00:38.507 --> 00:00:42.453
An error is the oven breaking down: a terminal signal.

14
00:00:42.453 --> 00:00:46.253
After it, nothing else arrives, not even an apology.

15
00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:49.197
Now the question that's easy to be shy about.

16
00:00:49.197 --> 00:00:54.103
If we catch that error, surely the stream carries on, and the 4 gets its turn?

17
00:00:54.233 --> 00:00:57.785
Well, the packer can't make a broken oven finish the tray.

18
00:00:57.785 --> 00:01:00.785
She can only decide what goes in the box instead.

19
00:01:00.785 --> 00:01:02.744
Reactor gives her three choices.

20
00:01:02.867 --> 00:01:07.297
One loaf from yesterday: onErrorReturn gives 8, 4, minus 1.

21
00:01:07.297 --> 00:01:12.704
The backup oven: onErrorResume switches to another belt, 8, 4, 100, 200.

22
00:01:12.704 --> 00:01:19.612
Or a clearer note: onErrorMap turns the error into a better one, and the stream still fails.

23
00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:20.690
Look for the 4.

24
00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:22.221
It isn't in any of them.

25
00:01:22.221 --> 00:01:27.516
Each operator caught the note on its way down, and replaced the rest of the stream.

26
00:01:27.516 --> 00:01:29.940
The oven above it had already stopped.

27
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.236
Error operators replace what's left.

28
00:01:32.236 --> 00:01:33.576
They never resume it.

29
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.034
So what about trying again?

30
00:01:36.034 --> 00:01:41.300
Every morning, the bakery orders flour from a mill, a service on another computer.

31
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:42.970
Usually it says CONFIRMED.

32
00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:46.888
Sometimes it's busy, and a minute later the same order works.

33
00:01:46.888 --> 00:01:50.034
And sometimes it refuses: no rye flour this week.

34
00:01:50.034 --> 00:01:52.025
Asking again won't change that.

35
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:55.304
For the busy mill, we want another try.

36
00:01:55.304 --> 00:01:57.205
retry doesn't rewind anything.

37
00:01:57.205 --> 00:02:05.568
When an error reaches it, it subscribes again: it signs a fresh order form, and the whole order is baked again, from the first loaf.

38
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.564
Here the oven picture breaks.

39
00:02:07.564 --> 00:02:10.391
A repaired oven carries on where it stopped.

40
00:02:10.391 --> 00:02:13.989
A retried Flux starts over, so rye-1 may be boxed twice.

41
00:02:13.989 --> 00:02:17.588
For one flour order, starting over is just what we want.

42
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.173
Let's count.

43
00:02:19.173 --> 00:02:21.936
retry(2), on a mill that always fails.

44
00:02:21.936 --> 00:02:24.336
How many calls does the mill get?

45
00:02:26.933 --> 00:02:27.335
Three.

46
00:02:27.335 --> 00:02:34.435
Two retries means three attempts, all within a millisecond or two, and then the original error: mill down.

47
00:02:34.567 --> 00:02:39.010
Three calls in one millisecond, to a mill that's already struggling.

48
00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:41.624
That's not a second try, that's a crowd.

49
00:02:41.624 --> 00:02:42.800
So we want pauses.

50
00:02:42.933 --> 00:02:55.984
With its random part switched off, Retry.backoff(3, 100 milliseconds) waits before each retry, twice as long each time: 100, then 200, then 400.

51
00:02:55.984 --> 00:03:01.059
So the calls go out at 0, 100, 300 and 700 milliseconds.

52
00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:04.411
Four attempts, because 3 counts the retries.

53
00:03:04.411 --> 00:03:14.513
At 700 milliseconds it gives up with RetryExhaustedException, Retries exhausted: 3/3, and the mill's last error rides along as its cause.

54
00:03:14.633 --> 00:03:17.685
Add a filter, and only the busy error is retried.

55
00:03:17.685 --> 00:03:19.678
A refusal goes straight through.

56
00:03:19.678 --> 00:03:27.773
And keep the random jitter in production, so a thousand bakeries that failed together don't all call back in the same millisecond.

57
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:29.212
Now a trap.

58
00:03:29.212 --> 00:03:36.658
Some clients call the mill the moment you call the method, and hand you a Mono holding the answer they already got.

59
00:03:36.658 --> 00:03:38.147
Retry that three times.

60
00:03:38.147 --> 00:03:40.089
How many calls reach the mill?

61
00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:42.980
One.

62
00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:46.972
The demo printed: mill busy, after 4 subscriptions.

63
00:03:46.972 --> 00:03:50.025
Each retry read the same stored answer.

64
00:03:50.133 --> 00:03:54.473
It's asking the same note, four times, if the mill is still busy.

65
00:03:54.473 --> 00:03:57.144
The note isn't going to change its mind.

66
00:03:57.144 --> 00:04:01.951
So make the call lazy: Mono.defer makes every subscription a fresh call.

67
00:04:02.667 --> 00:04:05.395
The hardest failure sends nothing at all.

68
00:04:05.395 --> 00:04:08.257
The mill takes the order and never answers.

69
00:04:08.257 --> 00:04:09.455
No item, no error.

70
00:04:09.455 --> 00:04:13.381
retry needs an error to react to, so nothing ever gives up.

71
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.413
So we set a kitchen timer.

72
00:04:15.413 --> 00:04:22.109
timeout(500 milliseconds) turns silence into a TimeoutException, and cancels the slow call.

73
00:04:22.109 --> 00:04:29.541
In the demo, a 100 millisecond timer on a 300 millisecond call rang, and the slow call was cancelled.

74
00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:32.440
Where you put the timer matters.

75
00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:39.366
Above retryWhen, each attempt gets its own 500 milliseconds, and a timeout is one more error to retry.

76
00:04:39.366 --> 00:04:42.489
Below it, it's one budget for the whole order.

77
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:43.581
One last thing.

78
00:04:43.581 --> 00:04:46.130
A timer only means you stopped waiting.

79
00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:48.287
It doesn't mean the mill said no.

80
00:04:48.287 --> 00:04:51.752
A real kitchen timer lets you open the oven and look.

81
00:04:51.752 --> 00:04:53.779
timeout can't see the far side.

82
00:04:53.779 --> 00:05:00.773
So send the same order number every time, and when the retries run out, mark the order pending, not failed.

83
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:09.443
Here's the whole order in Java and Kotlin: a lazy call, a timer per attempt, a filtered backoff, and pending at the end.

84
00:05:10.167 --> 00:05:13.242
So, does catching an error carry the stream on?

85
00:05:13.242 --> 00:05:13.439
No.

86
00:05:13.439 --> 00:05:18.609
An error ends it, and error operators only choose what goes in the box instead.

87
00:05:18.609 --> 00:05:23.582
retry bakes the whole order again, so make the call lazy, pause, and filter.

88
00:05:23.582 --> 00:05:26.200
And a timer turns silence into an error.

89
00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:27.167
Quick check.

90
00:05:27.167 --> 00:05:34.105
Retry.backoff(3, 50 milliseconds), no jitter, and a mill that's busy twice, then says CONFIRMED.

91
00:05:34.105 --> 00:05:37.285
When is the mill called, and what comes out?

92
00:05:39.867 --> 00:05:43.932
At 0, 50 and 150 milliseconds, and then CONFIRMED.

93
00:05:43.932 --> 00:05:47.347
Once an attempt works, the retrying stops.
